From 7594ceaef6dffb2abdcf76aecbef2ecd4cb7f831 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:41:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(parser): reuse one delimiter-run buffer across inline
 contexts
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Inline parsing built a fresh delimiter vector for every context — every
paragraph, heading, table cell, link text and image alt — so each one
holding a single `*`, `_`, `~` or `==` run paid an arena allocation, and a
context past four runs paid bumpalo's grow-and-copy ladder on top of it.
The runs are dead the moment pairing is done and nothing outside pairing
reads them, so the buffer is now the parser's: a context borrows it when it
records its first run and parks it again when it ends, cleared on the next
take. The slot holding it is shared with sub-parsers, and allocated in the
arena on the first run a parse records or the first sub-parser it builds —
a list of items or a run of quotes parses all of its inline content through
sub-parsers, so starting those with an empty slot would have kept one
buffer per item. A parse that neither pairs emphasis nor opens a container
still allocates nothing, and `Parser` grows by one pointer.

A context that records no run — most headings, list items and short
paragraphs — never asks for a buffer at all. The `Option` that answers that
is also the emptiness check which used to gate pairing, so the pairing
call, its linked-list walk and the two depth reads it opens with are all
skipped on the `None`.

Pairing itself is untouched: the buffer arrives empty, entries are pushed,
linked and retired in the same order as before, and a nested context (link
text, an image alt, an inline note's sub-source) that starts while the
context around it holds the buffer gets its own, so no context ever sees
another's runs; whichever of the two can hold more runs stays parked.

Arena bytes over one pre-sized chunk, against `fe8987c4`: 500 emphasis-dense
paragraphs 1,903,592 to 737,960, 500 list items 559,464 to 351,912, 500
block quotes 627,488 to 419,936, 500 paragraphs with emphasis inside link
text 564,048 to 356,496; prose without runs and a single 4,000-run
paragraph are unchanged. The rendered output is unchanged as well:
`tests/emphasis_pairing.rs` pins the nested shapes, and the CommonMark and
GFM suites, the snapshots, a
differential render of the 180 bundled documents in both profiles and 3,000
random emphasis-heavy inputs are byte-identical to `fe8987c4`.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
